[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Functions, Overloads, and Signatures in TypeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript elevates standard JavaScript functions by adding static types, allowing developers to define explicit parameter types and return types. Beyond basic typing, TypeScript supports function overloads, enabling a single function to accept different sets of arguments and return corresponding types."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Function Typing"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In TypeScript, you can specify the types of parameters directly in the function signature, as well as the return type of the function itself."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"function add(a: number, b: number): number {
    return a + b;
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Function Signatures and Overloads"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes a function can accept different types of inputs and return different types of outputs depending on those inputs. Function overloads allow you to supply multiple distinct function signatures for a single function implementation."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Overload signatures define the possible ways a function can be called."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The implementation signature contains the actual code and must be compatible with all overload signatures."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"function getValue(value: string): string;
function getValue(value: number): number;
function getValue(value: string | number): string | number {
    if (typeof value === "string") {
        return "String: " + value;
    }
    return value * 2;
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Overloads"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always order your overload signatures from most specific to least specific."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep the implementation signature hidden from consumers by ensuring the overloads cover all expected use cases safely."},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"By leveraging function overloads and strict signatures, you ensure robust compile-time safety and self-documenting code across your entire TypeScript application."}]
